  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">TZ FarmAbility - Economic Empowerment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project aims to contribute to the reduction of unemployment for 55 beneficiaries who are Young Mothers by creating an entrepreneurship opportunity of breeding and rearing 300 local ducks for meat and eggs in Musoma Council for a period of 15 months. Only those with an income of less than one dollar and no other economic opportunities due to the effects of the Covid 19 disease or any valence history are eligible. The indirect beneficiaries will reach another 200 people living in the Musoma Council&apos;s 16 wards. The youngmothers will be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge of the best scientific methods of duck rearing, such as quality breeding, products, nutrition, medications, shed balance, and marketing. For each admission phase, theory training will last onemonth and practical training will last 13 months. Beneficiaries of the project will be admitted in two stages. Local duck rearing is a low-cost business with high market demand. Ducks are birds in the area. Following the project, 70% of the duck species will be distributed free of charge to the direct beneficiaries, with the organization mentoring them by forming two groups for them to develop entrepreneurship in their own hands. 30% of the local ducks will be retained in the organization in order to sustain the project by reaching out to another 55 people in need after the project period has ended. Other stakeholders who will participate indirectly include a FarmABILITY team of four people, four local government leaders, four politicians, three bird watchers, and two livestock officers. Monitoring and evaluation will be carried out on a daily basis to track the project&apos;s progress. Seminars, workshops, media, and fieldwork are all activities that will help the project achieve its goal. Similarly, FarmABILITY will purchase equipment and rent project space, as well as construct a shed, purchase breeding ducks, food, and vaccines, and pay expert fees.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FarmABILITY began as an informal group in 2018 and will be legally established as an NGO on August 31, 2020, in compliance with theUnited Republic of Tanzania&apos;s statute on social organizations enacted in 2002. OSNET has 24 members, including kids, adults, and women, and FarmABILITY&apos;s main headquarters are in Musoma, in the Mara area. FarmABILITY began by conducting economic and social activities for vulnerable populations such as mothers, the elderly, young people, the crippled, and victims of abuse. FarmABILITY&apos;s current projects include vegetable cultivation grants for 15 poor and widowed women in 2021, as well as a project to establish food vending capacity for 60 young moms who were dismissed from school due to pregnancy before the law was altered in 2021. So far, FarmABILITYhas continued to carry out its responsibilities to bring about social change, such as raising awareness, altering attitudes, generating opportunities for jobs, and defending the rights and inclusion of all people. FarmABILITY&apos;smission is to create a society in which people with special needs may fully participate in agriculture. Transparency, human rights, respect, accountability, teamwork, networking, secrecy, and trust are among our core principles. FarmABILITY is pleased toannounce the completion of its proposal to construct restaurants for 12 Musoma Municipal Council girls who were dismissed from school after becoming pregnant before the law was altered in 2021. It should be mentioned that this is the first project launchedwhen FarmABILITY was established in 2020. Girls They were able to study all forms of catering and hotel administration through the restaurant, including cooking, customer service, budgeting, and marketing. Until now, the restaurant has become well-known in the community under the moniker FarmABILITY the BEST FOOD andhas brought about changes in the lives of the girls, such as the ability to expand their education and abilities as a result of earning their own money.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FarmABILITY</narrative>
